wasi-sh: read the "#!" line, and an `env` that does not exec.

Applied AFTER ash-hostbuiltin.patch, which it depends on: the slash short
circuit it hooks is the one that patch stopped from killing the shell, and
the interpreter it re-dispatches usually lands on CMDHOST.

On Linux a "#!" line is resolved by execve(), in the kernel. There is no
kernel here and no exec of any kind, so `./vendor/bin/phpunit` was a plain
127 even with every piece it needs registered. Two things had to change
together, because either alone is useless:

  1. evalcommand()'s prefix loop reads the "#!" line of a slashed name that
     resolved to nothing and splices the interpreter in front of the command,
     by BASENAME — /usr/bin/env, /bin/env and env are one command here, and
     none of those directories exist. Resolving the interpreter as a bare
     name is also what makes recursion impossible: it can never take the
     slash short circuit again.

  2. `env` becomes a shell builtin (CONFIG_ENV=n in build/busybox.config).
     The applet ends in execve() == ENOSYS, so `#!/usr/bin/env php` — how
     every PHP project spells its interpreter — resolved to a command that
     could not run anything. As a builtin it strips itself the way `command`
     does and hands the command word back to find_command(), which is the
     one place that reaches functions, builtins, applets and host builtins.

`env` is the only route back into shebang_expand(), and only for a file
called `env` whose own "#!" line names env; that one is refused rather than
recursed, with 126 and a message naming the file.

What counts as executable is A READABLE FILE WHOSE FIRST TWO BYTES ARE "#!".
The shim FS carries permission bits but does not enforce them and there is
no chmod, so test_exec() always fails and no other rule is available. See
ARCHITECTURE.md, which this patch's behaviour supersedes.

--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
